<h2>Context</h2>

<p>Haus Couture is a UK premium streetwear label. I designed and built its first custom storefront, then moved the same project to Shopify when production commerce requirements outgrew the bespoke implementation.</p>

<p>The important decision was not choosing one platform over another. It was recognizing that the right technical approach had changed.</p>

<CaseStudyMedia image={homeDesktop} alt="Haus Couture production storefront homepage with a dark streetwear campaign hero" caption="01 / The production Shopify storefront today." />

<h2>Phase one: custom storefront</h2>

<p>The project began as a custom Astro site. I owned the visual direction, information architecture, responsive layout and Vercel deployment. This phase established the brand’s dark, direct storefront language while the product range was still being organized.</p>

<CaseStudyMedia image={archived} alt="Archived custom Haus Couture Astro storefront with a restrained editorial layout" caption="02 / The archived Astro implementation that established the first visual direction." label="archived custom build" />

<h2>The decision</h2>

<p>Once the commercial requirements were concrete, the project needed mature checkout, payments, policy handling, catalogue administration and an operational backend the client could manage. Rebuilding that infrastructure would have consumed engineering time without making the brand more distinctive.</p>

<WorkflowDiagram
  title="Architecture changed with the requirement"
  footer="The design work carried forward. The commerce infrastructure moved to the platform built to own it."
  nodes={[
