/>

<h2>Phase two: Shopify production</h2>

<p>On Shopify, my work covers the storefront and store configuration: theme customization, product and variant structure, collections, navigation, policy content, responsive presentation, domain integration and launch.</p>

<CaseStudyMedia image={collection} alt="Haus Couture hoodies collection showing the dark product catalogue" caption="03 / One of six collection views organizing the catalogue." />

<CaseStudyMedia image={productPage} alt="Haus Couture product page with garment photography, size selection and GBP pricing" caption="04 / A production product page with variants, pricing and checkout path." />

<p>Shopify supplies the commerce platform, checkout and payment infrastructure. I do not present those platform capabilities as custom engineering. My contribution is the custom first phase, the product and visual direction, the Shopify storefront configuration, and taking the store to production.</p>

<h2>Result</h2>

<p>The live store has 29 product listings across 11 style families and six collections, with a responsive GBP storefront and working commerce policies. The original custom deployment remains useful evidence of how the product direction evolved.</p>

<CaseStudyMedia image={homeMobile} alt="Haus Couture production storefront displayed on a mobile viewport" caption="05 / The live storefront on mobile." />

<h2>Lesson</h2>

<blockquote><p>Custom work should carry the brand. Mature infrastructure should carry the undifferentiated risk.</p></blockquote>

